SEG = 'PRV'
CAT = 'cbcsPrivacy'
Q = [
("Which signed form does HIPAA require before a practice sends PHI to a patient's insurer for payment?",
 'None is required', ['A HIPAA authorization', 'A consent to treat form', 'A signed privacy notice'],
 'Under 45 CFR 164.506, a provider may use and disclose PHI for treatment, payment and health care operations without an authorization. Getting consent for these uses is optional.'),
("Which disclosure needs the patient's signed authorization?",
 'Records for her life insurer', ['Claim data sent to her health plan', 'Notes sent to her referring doctor', 'Charts pulled for a coding audit'],
 'A life insurer underwriting a policy is not part of treatment, payment or operations, so 164.508 requires a valid authorization. Claims, referrals and internal audits fall under 164.506.'),
('A patient asks for her statements to go to her work address. What must the practice do?',
 'Honor her reasonable request', ['Ask her to explain in writing', 'Keep mailing to her home', 'Agree only if she pays a fee'],
 'Under 164.522(b), a provider must accommodate reasonable requests for confidential communications at another address and may not require the patient to explain why.'),
('A patient asks the office to limit which staff may see her chart. Must it agree?',
 'No, it may decline', ['Yes, every request binds it', 'Yes, once it is written', 'Only if her doctor agrees'],
 'Under 164.522(a), a covered entity is not required to agree to most restriction requests. The one it must accept is a restriction on disclosures to a health plan for care paid in full out of pocket.'),
("A workers' compensation carrier asks for records on a claimant's work injury. What does HIPAA allow?",
 'Release what the comp law requires', ['Release only with her authorization', 'Wait for a court order first', 'Send her whole medical record'],
 "Section 164.512(l) permits disclosures authorized by workers' compensation law, limited to what that law requires. No authorization is needed."),
("An attorney's subpoena with no court order asks for records. What must the practice receive first?",
 'Assurance she had notice', ["The attorney's bar number", 'Copy fees paid in advance', "Her insurer's approval"],
 'Under 164.512(e), without a court order the requester must give satisfactory assurance that the patient was notified of the request, or that a qualified protective order was sought.'),
('Does the minimum necessary rule limit what a patient may get from her own record?',
 'No, it does not apply', ['Yes, to the last visit', 'Yes, unless she pays', 'Only for billing records'],
 'Section 164.502(b)(2) says minimum necessary does not apply to disclosures to the individual who is the subject of the information.'),
('A biller sends records to support an appeal of one denied visit. What should she send?',
 'Notes for that date of service', ['The complete medical record', 'Every visit from the past year', 'Visit notes plus all lab history'],
 'The minimum necessary standard in 164.502(b) applies to payment disclosures. Only the documentation for the denied service supports the appeal.'),
("A billing clerk opens a neighbor's account with no work reason. How does HIPAA treat it?",
 'Presumed breach to assess', ['Fine, since nothing was copied', 'Fine, if no one complains', 'A matter for office policy only'],
 'Under 164.402, an impermissible use of PHI is presumed to be a breach unless a risk assessment shows a low probability the information was compromised.'),
('Which law made business associates directly liable for HIPAA compliance?',
 'HITECH Act', ['Stark Law', 'Privacy Act of 1974', 'Affordable Care Act'],
 'The HITECH Act of 2009 extended HIPAA Security Rule duties and penalties directly to business associates, which the 2013 Omnibus Rule put into effect.'),
('After a patient signs an authorization the practice asked for, what must the practice give her?',
 'A copy of what she signed', ['A refund of any fees', 'A new privacy notice', 'A list of past disclosures'],
 'Under 164.508(c)(4), when a covered entity seeks an authorization, it must give the individual a copy of the signed authorization.'),
('How may a patient revoke a HIPAA authorization she signed?',
 'In writing, at any time', ['Orally, at the front desk', 'Only before it is used', 'Only with a new form'],
 'Section 164.508(b)(5) lets an individual revoke an authorization at any time in writing, except where the entity has already acted on it.'),
('By when must a provider first give a patient its Notice of Privacy Practices?',
 'No later than the first service', ['Only if the patient asks', 'With the first billing statement', 'Within 60 days of the visit'],
 'Section 164.520(c) requires a direct treatment provider to give the notice no later than the date of first service delivery, and to make a good faith effort to get a written acknowledgment.'),
('May a provider refuse treatment unless the patient signs an authorization for marketing?',
 'No, care cannot depend on it', ['Yes, for any authorization', 'Yes, if stated in the notice', 'Only for new patients'],
 'Under 164.508(b)(4), a covered entity may not condition treatment, payment, enrollment or eligibility on signing an authorization, except in narrow research and insurer cases.'),
('A patient brings her husband to discuss her balance and does not object. What may staff do?',
 'Discuss the bill with both', ['Require his authorization', 'Ask him to leave the room', 'Explain it only by letter'],
 "Section 164.510(b) lets a provider share information relevant to a family member's involvement in paying for care when the patient is present and does not object."),
('A practice uses patient case notes to train new coders. Which HIPAA purpose is this?',
 'Health care operations', ['Treatment of patients', 'Payment for services', 'Marketing to patients'],
 'Under the 164.501 definition, health care operations include training programs in which practitioners or staff learn under supervision. Treatment and payment are the other two routine purposes.'),
]
